    "profile_text": {
        "body": "NVIDIA is a public company, traded as NVDA and registered with CIK 0001045810, founded in 1993 and headquartered in Santa Clara, California. Its most recent 10-K, covering the period ending 2026-01-25, reported revenue of $215,938,000,000.\n\nOn its own site, NVIDIA describes Nemotron as a family of multimodal, open AI models built for long-running, self-evolving agents, aimed at agentic reasoning in graduate-level science, advanced math, and visual understanding. The company says the models are trained on transparent data and supported across platforms including NVIDIA RTX PRO and NVIDIA DGX Spark. Named pieces include Nemotron 3.5 Lightning for specialized tasks, NeMo Switchyard for routing each step to a model, Nemotron Speech models for automatic speech recognition, text-to-speech, and neural machine translation, and Nemotron Retriever models for document understanding and embeddings. NVIDIA publishes model weights, training datasets, and training techniques under the NVIDIA Open Model License, which it describes as permissive and permitting commercial deployment without crediting NVIDIA. The models are also offered as NVIDIA NIM microservices, which the company says require an NVIDIA AI Enterprise license and can be tried or downloaded from build.nvidia.com. Related tooling named on the page includes NVIDIA NeMo, NVIDIA Blueprints, NVIDIA Dynamo, and TensorRT-LLM, alongside open-source libraries SGLang and vLLM. Bryan Catanzaro, VP of applied deep learning research at NVIDIA, is quoted on the vision behind Nemotron, and ServiceNow is cited among partners working on the models.\n\nThe company's tracked AI products are NVIDIA Nemotron and NVIDIA NIM. Litigation exposure is recorded in California, with 281 cases.",
